conversion/deepseek41.py: carry the Engram tables as MXFP8 instead of BF16 (K100AI).

The Strata engine's Engram reader takes MXFP8, Q6_K and IQ4_NL, not BF16: a model converted by
upstream's converter loads its dense weights, opens the Engram layer and then stops with
"engram.embed.weight type BF16 is not one the Engram reader handles".

The checkpoint's Engram tables are e4m3 with one e8m0 scale per 32 elements, which is exactly an
MXFP8 block, so they are copied over losslessly.  The block order is the engine's: [scale][32 codes]
(ds4_engram_io.hpp unpacks the scale from byte 0), not [32 codes][scale].  Upstream's code dequantizes
them to BF16 (2 bytes a value, twice the file).  gguf-py has no MXFP8 id, so the converter registers
llama.cpp's own id (43) with its block size before writing.

diff --git a/conversion/deepseek41.py b/conversion/deepseek41.py
--- a/conversion/deepseek41.py
+++ b/conversion/deepseek41.py
@@ -14,6 +14,17 @@
 
 from .base import LazyTorchTensor, ModelBase, TextModel, gguf
 from .deepseek import DeepseekV4FlashVisionModel, DeepseekV4Model
+
+# K100AI: llama.cpp's gguf-py carries no MXFP8 id (only MXFP4), but the Strata engine's Engram reader accepts
+# MXFP8, Q6_K and IQ4_NL - not BF16.  The checkpoint's Engram tables are e4m3 with one e8m0 scale per 32
+# elements, which is exactly MXFP8's block, so they can be carried over losslessly as 32 value bytes + 1 scale
+# byte.  Register the id (llama.cpp's own 43) with its block size so the writer can emit it.
+_MXFP8_GGML_ID = 43
+try:
+    from gguf.constants import GGML_QUANT_SIZES as _GGML_QUANT_SIZES
+    _GGML_QUANT_SIZES[_MXFP8_GGML_ID] = (32, 33)
+except Exception:   # pragma: no cover - a gguf-py without constants
+    pass
 
 
